# Plan task schema

This rule owns the executable task schema, the final verification task and plan
fidelity. Stage boundaries, delegation, the Domain map, guidance maps, the planning
artifact layout and user-flow handoffs are in `{CURSOR}/rules/plan-workflow.mdc`; a
task's Agent is one selection from that Domain map.

## Task schema

New plans use this eleven-field Workstream schema, with no execution-model marker or
version label. Schema shape grants no Git-write authority: task commits need explicit
approval in Decisions. Never reinterpret or retrofit an approved plan during Build;
a contract migration needs an explicitly reviewed revision.

Every implementation and final verification todo gets a task artifact and a plan
section. Todo ids are lowercase kebab-case. Each todo's content names
`{CURSOR}/skills/implement-plan/SKILL.md`, its todo id and its task artifact.
The plan section points to the artifact:

```markdown
### <todo-id>
- Task artifact: [Task 01 — <purpose>](tasks/01-task-<plan-slug>.md)
```

The artifact has a stable descriptive H1 in the repository's commit subject
convention, read from its recent history; its text without `# ` is the verified
commit subject. The artifact path and todo id are its durable identity, not the
ordinal. The task section:

```markdown
## <todo-id>
- Workstream: <named organizational group>
- Agent: <one selection from the Domain map>
- Discovery: <relative links to relevant discovery files and headings>
- Read first: <canonical guidance, selected references, and required sources>
- Owns: <exact implementation files, or none for final verification>
- Depends on: <todo ids or none>
- Completion depends on: <todo ids or none>
- Contracts: <approved definitions or exact references, or none>
- Resources: <repositories this task commits to, and shared runtimes or fixtures its checks use, or none>
- Done when: <observable outcome>
- Verification (Lead runs): <exact commands and required results>
```

Add task detail only when it helps execution: consumed and produced contracts,
existing patterns, required wiring, fixture setup and preservation. The plan's Decisions
override task detail; a contradiction blocks readiness.

**Workstream** is a non-empty descriptive display group, not an ordinal or a
prerequisite, and need not equal a domain. Workstreams and ordinals never serialize
work. Keep each application task within one domain and settle producer/consumer
contracts before splitting; several tasks may share a domain Agent. Each layer's part
of a cross-layer change, such as a permission's seed data, backend authorization and
client guard, goes to that layer's task. Invalid grouping values, mixed grouping
fields and Agent/ownership mismatches are plan defects.

**Depends on** names Lead-verified start prerequisites; **Completion depends on**
names tasks that must be verified before this task's verification or Done, without
blocking its implementation. `none` is valid for either. The union of both edge types
has no unknown ids, self-edges or cycles. A physical generated artifact or shared
symbol that must exist first is a start prerequisite; a complete approved API
contract alone is not. Put mutual integration checks in final verification instead
of creating completion cycles. A task is completed only after Lead verification and
its commit, or a verified no-change/non-Git disposition; a worker's return is not Done.

**Contracts** identifies complete approved definitions (inline or by exact artifact
heading): producer and consumer tasks, names, types/shapes/nullability, behavior,
authorization/errors and evidence as applicable. There is no second mutable contract
store. An unresolved consequential contract blocks readiness; consumers use the fixed
definition, never a sibling's moving source. A contract change during Build follows
the reviewed-revision boundary.

**Owns** is the task's edit boundary. Every implementation file, including tests,
fixtures and shared registration/wiring (the DbContext file, the DI registration
file, a database project file, barrel `index.ts`, NgRx module/store wiring), has
exactly one owner. No two tasks own the same path or a parent/child of it, compared
after resolving tokens, separators and symlinks. Same-repository tasks run one at a
time (implementation skill, Admission); there is no other registration-file lock.
Owns may name a new EF Core migration as `Migrations/<timestamp>_<Name>.cs` and its
`.Designer.cs`, in the project's migrations folder, with the context's
`*ModelSnapshot.cs` by its actual name. The worker generates them with one
`dotnet ef migrations add <Name>`; before checking Owns or staging, the Lead
replaces `<timestamp>` with the generated value.

**Resources** names each repository the task commits to (non-Git outputs say so and
are never redirected into another repository) and any shared runtime or fixture its
checks use: database, API, Angular dev server, Tilt, publish/deploy.

The implementation skill owns admission, check ordering around writers and shared
runtimes, task commits and the ledger at
`{ARTIFACTS}/planning/<plan-slug>/execution-state.md`. Only the Lead writes the
ledger; it is excluded from frozen inputs and every Owns.

## Final verification

The last numbered task and last execution todo is end-to-end verification: Agent:
orchestrator, Owns: none, Depends on every preceding execution task, displayed as the
final integration gate. It links each discovery acceptance requirement and required
UF ID to exact checks of the integrated result, with runtime prerequisites and
required evidence; it is not a request for unrelated broad testing. The
implementation skill owns its execution, failure routing and completion.

A plan that adds or changes UI has a final-task section headed
`Browser verification: verification-agent`. Link each UF ID with a UI step and
declare flow grouping, ordering/independence, slots and evidence
requirements; do not duplicate flow definitions or add schema fields. Default to one
verifier; plan two only for independent journeys with separate fixtures and
demonstrated session/account isolation. Unknown independence or shared mutable state
serializes, and dependent cross-page journeys stay together. Non-UI plans need no
browser section.

## Plan fidelity

Build executes only plans in the eleven-field Workstream schema; any other format
stops with a request to re-plan. Unknown declarations, mixed fields and incomplete
schemas block execution.

Execute the approved artifact set without expanding Owns, changing contracts or
behavior, skipping tasks/dependencies, weakening verification, or silently rewriting
instructions. This overrides the scope gate's minor-substitution policy. The Lead
follows `implement-plan` for scheduling, correction limits, recovery and global
stops; workers follow `implementation-contract.md` for their own recovery and report.
Neither role may use recovery to redesign the plan.
